EXAMPLES.append({'id':'we-3-source-8','num':'8','title':'Example 8','problem':r'Differentiate (a) \(4x^3-2x^2+5x\), (b) \((x^2-2x)(x^3-3)\), (c) \(\frac{x^2+13x+9}{x^2+11x+3}\).','given':'A polynomial sum, a product of polynomials and a quotient of polynomials.','method':'Use the sum, product and quotient rules; verify the product by expanding and retain the quotient domain.','steps':[r'For (a), differentiate each term: \((4x^3)′=12x^2\), \((-2x^2)′=-4x\), and (5x)′ = 5. The sum is 12x² − 4x + 5.',r'For (b), choose u = x² − 2x and v = x³ − 3. Then u′ = 2x − 2 and v′ = 3x². The product rule gives uv′ + vu′.',r'Expand \((x^2-2x)3x^2+(x^3-3)(2x-2)=3x^4-6x^3+2x^4-2x^3-6x+6=5x^4-8x^3-6x+6\).',r'Check (b) independently: the original product is x⁵ − 2x⁴ − 3x² + 6x, whose derivative is the same 5x⁴ − 8x³ − 6x + 6.',r'For (c), let u = x² + 13x + 9, v = x² + 11x + 3, u′ = 2x + 13, v′ = 2x + 11. Use \((u/v)′=\frac{vu′-uv′}{v^2}\).',r'The first product is \((x^2+11x+3)(2x+13)=2x^3+35x^2+149x+39\). The second is \((x^2+13x+9)(2x+11)=2x^3+37x^2+161x+99\).',r'Subtract the whole second product: the numerator is −2x² − 12x − 60. Thus the derivative is \(\frac{-2x^2-12x-60}{(x^2+11x+3)^2}\).',r'Keep the original domain: x² + 11x + 3 ≠ 0, so \(x\ne\frac{-11\pm\sqrt{109}}2\). These excluded points do not acquire a derivative.'],'answer':r'(a) \(12x^2-4x+5\); (b) \(5x^4-8x^3-6x+6\); (c) \(\frac{-2x^2-12x-60}{(x^2+11x+3)^2}\), where the original denominator is nonzero.','sourceClarification':'The source has dy/du instead of dy/dx in (b). The final source simplification of (c) continues on page 75; the full answer above is independently calculated.','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':74,'jpg':'041_page_2.jpg','verification':'visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]})

