 heading('Example 9'),paragraph(r'The cost in (million) dollars to produce x units of wheat is given by \(C(x)=5000+20x+10\sqrt{x}\). Find the marginal cost, when (a) x = 9 units; (b) x = 16 units; (c) x = 25 units; (d) As more wheat is produced; what happens to the marginal cost?'),heading('Solution'),paragraph(r'a. If \(C(x)=5000+20x+10\sqrt{x}\), then the marginal cost is the derivative of C(x) with respect to x:'),command(None,r"\(C'(x)=20+10\left(\frac12\right)x^{-\frac12}=20+\frac5{\sqrt{x}}\)"),paragraph('The marginal cost at x = 9 units is obtained by inserting x = 9 in C′(x):'),command(None,r"\(C'(9)=20+\frac5{\sqrt9}=20+\frac53=\frac{65}3\approx\text{\$21.67}\)"),paragraph('b. The marginal cost at x = 16 units is obtained by inserting x = 16 in C′(x):'),command(None,r"\(C'(16)=20+\frac5{\sqrt{16}}=20+\frac54=\frac{85}4=\text{\$21.25}\)"),paragraph('c. The marginal cost at x = 25 units is obtained by inserting x = 25 in C′(x):'),command(None,r"\(C'(25)=20+\frac5{\sqrt{25}}=20+\frac55=\text{\$21}\)"),paragraph(r'd. It decreases and approaches \(\text{\$20}\).'),heading('Marginal Analysis'),paragraph('In business and economics the rates of change of such variables as cost, revenue and profit are most important. Economists use the word marginal to refer to rates of change. For example, the marginal cost refers to the rate of change of cost. Since the derivative of a function gives the rate of change of the function, the marginal cost (or revenue or profit) function is found by taking the derivative of the cost (or revenue or profit) function. The marginal cost at some level of production x is the cost to produce the (x + 1)st item (i.e., one more item).'),
 {'type':'clarification','text':r'The question measures C in millions of dollars, so C′ is in millions of dollars per production unit; the printed dollar labels omit that scale. The real-valued cost model has x ≥ 0, but its finite derivative requires x > 0. Marginal cost approximates the cost of one additional unit; the exact extra cost is \(C(x+1)-C(x)=20+10(\sqrt{x+1}-\sqrt{x})\), generally different from C′(x).'}
