EXAMPLES.append({'id':'we-3-source-9','num':'9','title':'Example 9','problem':r'The cost in millions of dollars for x units of wheat is \(C(x)=5000+20x+10\sqrt{x}\). Find marginal cost at x = 9, 16, 25 and its behavior as production increases.','given':'Cost in millions of dollars; production x ≥ 0.','method':'Differentiate for x > 0, evaluate the derivative and distinguish marginal cost from exact one-unit change.','steps':[r'For x > 0 and h ≠ 0, rationalize: \(\frac{\sqrt{x+h}-\sqrt{x}}h=\frac1{\sqrt{x+h}+\sqrt{x}}\). Its limit is 1/(2√x).',r'Thus \(C′(x)=0+20+10\cdot\frac1{2\sqrt{x}}=20+\frac5{\sqrt{x}}\).',r'At x = 9, \(C′(9)=20+5/3=65/3\approx21.67\) million dollars per production unit.',r'At x = 16, \(C′(16)=20+5/4=85/4=21.25\) million dollars per production unit.',r'At x = 25, \(C′(25)=20+5/5=21\) million dollars per production unit.',r'As x increases, 5/√x decreases toward zero. C′ decreases toward 20 million dollars per production unit, staying above 20 for finite x > 0.',r'The exact extra-unit cost is \(20+10(\sqrt{x+1}-\sqrt{x})=20+\frac{10}{\sqrt{x+1}+\sqrt{x}}\). At x = 9 this is about 21.6228 million dollars, compared with marginal cost about 21.6667 million dollars per unit.',r'At x = 0 the cost is defined, but the right-hand difference quotient includes 10/√h and is unbounded as h → 0⁺. The finite derivative requires x > 0.'],'answer':'(a) 65/3 ≈ 21.67; (b) 85/4 = 21.25; (c) 21 million dollars per production unit. (d) Decreases toward 20 million dollars per production unit as x → ∞.','sourceClarification':'The question states million-dollar costs but the solution prints ordinary dollar labels. Marginal cost approximates an additional-unit cost; the exact change is C(x + 1) − C(x).','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':75,'jpg':'042_page_1.jpg','verification':'complete question and solution visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]})

PAGES.append(page('043_page_1.jpg',77,[
 heading('3.4 Chain Rule'),paragraph('“The chain rule is a rule which we use to differentiate the composite functions”. We have learnt about composition of functions in unit-2 that a function is a composite functions of the two similar functions f(x) and g(x) if it is written as f[g(x)]. In other words it is a function of a function. For example sin(x²) is a composite functions because of we consider f(x) = sin(x) and g(x) = x² then f[g(x)] = sin(x²). Generally, we write chain rule as;'),command(None,r"\(\frac{d}{dx}[f(g(x))]=f'[g(x)]\cdot g'(x)\)",'i'),
 heading('3.4.1 Prove that dy/dx = (dy/du)(du/dx) when y = f(u) and u = g(x)'),heading('Proof'),paragraph('For our convenience, we set'),command(None,r'\(y=F(x)=f[g(x)]\)','i'),paragraph('Let u = g(x) the equation (i) will be'),command(None,r'\(y=F(x)=f[g(x)]=f(u)\)','ii'),command(None,r'\(y+\Delta y=F(x+\Delta x)\)','iii'),paragraph('Now, subtracting equation (i) from equation (iii)'),command(None,r'\(y+\Delta y-y=F(x+\Delta x)-F(x)\)'),command(None,r'\(\Delta y=F(x+\Delta x)-F(x)\)','iv'),paragraph('Equation (iv) can be written as'),command(None,r'\(\Delta y=f[g(x+\Delta x)]-f[g(x)]\)','v'),paragraph('Where,'),command(None,r'\(\Delta u=g(x+\Delta x)-g(x)\)'),command(None,r'\(g(x+\Delta x)=\Delta u+g(x)\)','vi'),paragraph('Substitute the value of g(x + Δx) from equation (vi) to equation (v)'),command(None,r'\(\Delta y=f[\Delta u+g(x)]-f[g(x)]\)'),command(None,r'\(F(x+\Delta x)-F(x)=f(u+\Delta u)-f(u),\quad u=g(x)\)'),paragraph('Divide equation (iv) by Δx then we have'),command(None,r'\(\frac{\Delta y}{\Delta x}=\frac{F(x+\Delta x)-F(x)}{\Delta x}=\frac{f(u+\Delta u)-f(u)}{\Delta x}\)','vii'),paragraph('Multiply and divide the right side of equation by Δu so,'),command(None,r'\(\frac{\Delta y}{\Delta x}=\frac{f(u+\Delta u)-f(u)}{\Delta u}\cdot\frac{\Delta u}{\Delta x}\)'),command(None,r'\(=\frac{f(u+\Delta u)-f(u)}{\Delta u}\cdot\frac{g(x+\Delta x)-g(x)}{\Delta x}\)','viii'),paragraph('Apply limit Δx → 0 and limit Δu → 0 in equation (viii)'),command(None,r'\(\lim_{\Delta x\to0}\frac{\Delta y}{\Delta x}=\lim_{\Delta u\to0}\frac{f(u+\Delta u)-f(u)}{\Delta u}\cdot\lim_{\Delta x\to0}\frac{g(x+\Delta x)-g(x)}{\Delta x}\)'),command(None,r'\(\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{dy}{du}\cdot\frac{du}{dx}\)'),paragraph('It can also written as:'),command(None,r"\(\frac{d}{dx}[f(g(x))]=f'(u)g'(x)=f'[g(x)]g'(x)\)"),
 {'type':'clarification','text':r'The functions need to be composable, with g differentiable at x and f differentiable at u = g(x). Their types need not be “similar”. Differentiability of g implies continuity, so Δu → 0 as Δx → 0. The printed cancellation assumes Δu ≠ 0, which need not hold for every nonzero Δx. To cover zero increments, define q(k) = [f(u + k) − f(u)]/k for k ≠ 0, and q(0) = f′(u). Then q(k) → f′(u) and, for every Δx ≠ 0, the composite difference quotient is q(Δu)·Δu/Δx, including when Δu = 0. Its limit is f′(g(x))g′(x).'},
 heading('Example 10'),paragraph(r'Differentiate the following functions w.r.t. x: (a) \(f(x)=(4x-3)^3\); (b) \(f(x)=\sqrt{15x^2+1}\).'),paragraph('The printed solutions continue on page 78.')
]))
