 heading('3.4.3 Use of chain rule to show the generalized power rule'),command(None,r"\(\frac{d}{dx}[f(x)]^n=n[f(x)]^{n-1}f'(x)\)"),heading('Proof'),paragraph(r'Let \(y=[f(x)]^n\) and u = f(x) then y = uⁿ and \(\frac{dy}{du}=nu^{n-1}\) (by power rule)'),command(None,r'\(\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{dy}{du}\frac{du}{dx}=nu^{n-1}\frac{du}{dx}\)'),command(None,r"\(\frac{d}{dx}[f(x)]^n=n[f(x)]^{n-1}f'(x),\quad\frac{du}{dx}=f'(x)\)"),paragraph('In words, if f(x) is equal to an expression in x raised to a power of n, then f′(x) is equal to the product of n times the expression to the n−1 power times the derivative of the expression with respect to the variable. The statement is known as the general power rule.'),
 {'type':'clarification','text':'The notation reuses f for the inner expression and for the full powered function in the verbal statement. More clearly, if Y = [u(x)]ⁿ, then Y′ = n[u(x)]ⁿ⁻¹u′(x). The rule requires u differentiable and the outer power differentiable at the inner value. Positive integer powers work for all real inner values; negative powers require a nonzero value. Noninteger powers need their appropriate real domain and endpoint conditions; positive inner values are a sufficient setting. For n = 0, the constant function 1 has derivative zero without using an undefined 0·u⁻¹ at u = 0.'},
